Article 28 The following acts that damage wetlands and their ecological functions shall be prohibited:
(1) Reclaiming (enclosing and reclaiming) or draining natural wetlands, or permanently cutting off the water source of natural wetlands;
(2) Unauthorizedly filling natural wetlands, or unauthorizedly extracting sand, mining, or taking soil;
(3) Discharging industrial wastewater, domestic sewage, or other waste water or sewage that does not meet the discharge standards for water pollutants, or dumping, piling up, discarding, or scattering solid waste;
(4) Overgrazing or excessive gathering of wild plants; overfishing or exterminatory fishing; over-fertilization, over-application of pesticides, or over-dosing of bait, and other planting and aquaculture behaviors that pollute wetlands;
(5) Other acts that damage wetlands and their ecological functions.

Article 46 When conducting supervision and inspection, the competent departments of forestry and grassland, natural resources, water administration, housing and urban-rural construction, ecological environment, and agriculture and rural affairs under the people's governments at or above the county level shall have the authority to take the following measures:
(1) Inquire of the unit or individual under inspection and require them to provide explanations on matters related to the supervision and inspection;
(2) Conduct on-site inspection;
(3) Consult and copy relevant documents and materials, and seal up documents and materials that are likely to be transferred, destroyed, concealed, or tampered with;
(4) Seal up or seize the premises, facilities, or property suspected of being involved in illegal activities.

Article 63 For the purposes of this Law, the following terms mean:
(1) "Mangrove wetlands" mean the offshore and coastal intertidal wetlands mainly composed of mangrove plants;
(2) "Peat bog wetlands" mean the marsh wetlands where peat has developed.
